eir own right which  was an amazing achievement for a  resistance movement i mean  wreck your brains and try to think about  another resistance movement of that kind  that has one government outright in its  own  in its own way  um  having achieved that  it then  such was the anger in fata fata's  inability  to accept defeat  um  fatter's  sense of entitlement it had been there  for so long and in many ways fatter was  run and arafat was  to blame for much of this fatah was run  just as most of the  autocratic arab regimes are run  snouts in the trough for the family the  friends the tribes uh for the  organization others who weren't  in the network were kept out  that worked for jobs it worked for  running the government it worked for  liaising with the international  community it worked for the spending of  huge international funds  so  such was the anger  that  abbas  on the advice of washington that has to  be said  stripped tried to strip much of the  power out of the uh  the ministries of the  hamas government before it took office  so that it would be less strong so that  all of the power would be invested in  abbas as president  so there is so much hatred so much anger  so much ill will  and yet at the bottom  each side recognizes two things  that it has to do a deal that there has  to be an agreement between them  and perhaps sadly more often than not  they recognize that that deal is too  difficult to achieve at the moment  so yes they are played against each  other but they're playing into israel's  hands on this  really the same question restated given  the factual uh the fractured state of  the various palestinian organizations is  the continued existence of the state of  israel secure  depends on who you ask  if you go to the democrat demographic  argument no  if you go to the demographic argument  that uh says that in as soon as 15 20 25  years there will be an arab majority  in what is now the state of israel and  the occupied territories it's very hard  to see how the jewish state of israel  will be able to argue  that it is the you know the purest  democracy in the middle east it will  cease to be that  and that would be a significant  wound for it  look at it another way one of the  significant achievements of the whole  israel venture  is that since 1940 i have a colleague  a newspaper editor who when she briefs  reporters she says on a particular sort  of story that she wants delivered she  says the hand goes up here give me the  helicopter view  she wants she wants what it looks like  from up here  so if you look from up here  1948  1967 first intifada second intifada  palestinian elections  it doesn't matter  it doesn't matter who's in government in  israel  who's in government in washington  whether there's any sort of peace  process on foot whether there's the  violent interaction of interfada whether  people are talking or not talking  what has happened  between 1948 and the present is  that israel has succeeded not only in  holding what it had  but  what it took  and is holding that too  and notwithstanding the requirements of  the geneva convention has resettled the  occupied territories with its own people  has increased the  scope of those settlements vertically as  we say and horizontally has increased  the mileage of roads linking them has  increased the number of checkpoints that  limit palestinian movement around them  so  in terms of  setting up the state of israel  and the famous words of ben gurion after  the declaration of independence that we  shouldn't worry about establishing  borders now because we don't know how  far we're going to be able to push them  out  that's still an ongoing operation and to  that extent israel is winning  but whether it can sustain itself in its  winning  is a moot point i mean i often i often  see the israelis and the palestinians as  inevitable prisoners of each other  we have five minutes for two important  questions one  the shorter question or the shorter  answer what is the future of hamas  i think hamas is a a viable organization  it's a more viable organization than  fata  um i think fatah has demonstrated again  and again that  it can't  uh survive as it is and it can't change  itself in order to survive and the more  that the west does and the more that  israel does to prop it up um that code  word that you'll see often in media  reports is bolster it we've got to  bolster hamas sorry we've got a bolster  arafat  and  and  mahmoud abbas  but in bolstering him he is humiliated  time and again in the eyes of the  palestinian people he was humiliated  over the retreat to the perimeter of  gaza in 2005 that was a golden  opportunity for the israelis to show  that he was their partner had he been  brought into a a dialogue instead of  attack happening as the result of  unilateral negotiation he might have  been able to present himself as  achieving something for the palestinian  people instead hamas jumped up and down  and said we drove the israelis out  aren't we great and most palestinians  believe that  more recently the provision of weapons  for  his security forces to fight hamas  israel felt obliged to reveal publicly  that they were providing weapons to fata  to fight hamas think of how that went  down in palestinian public opinion  our last question solicits your personal  opinion  is a negotiated settlement of the  palestinian issue to state or otherwise  possible  i have ceased to believe that israel and  the palestinian people can negotiate a  solution themselves i think after 60  years of most unbelievable violence  most unbelievable privation most  unbelievable insecurity amongst israelis  i believe both sides are so punch drunk  now that they're incapable of  agreeing or negotiating a settlement  the only settlement i see coming is one  that would be imposed and i have to say  i don't see that happening anytime soon  we are out of time on on behalf of the  world affairs council and our audience i  would like to thank mr paul nego for  providing us with his thoughts and  insights he has agreed to stay and sign  copies of his book it's excellent i've  read it it not only covers the political  uh  issues he's talked about but it reads  like the spy novel that it is so i would  encourage you to try it out thank you  very much  [Music]  very  is the former editor for the sydney  morning herald he has reported from many  hot spots in the world including  afghanistan and iraq he's the author of  manhattan to baghdad dispatches from the  front line in the war on terror and in  baghdad of reporters war  paul mcgowan is chief correspondent for  the sydney morning herald to read his  reporting visit smh.com.a.u  here at the conservative political  action committee of samuel wertzelbacher  also known as joe the plumber out with a  new book mr wurtzel backer what's your  book about  it's about
 

